    "message": "docs(desktop): document the desktop app across README, USAGE and the Guide\n\nSix releases landed in one day -- the window, the window picker, nine settings,\npersistence and a Windows installer fix -- and none of it was in the\nuser-facing docs. Anyone reading README would not know mcc-desktop had a window\nat all.\n\nREADME gains a Desktop App section covering the three server modes, the window\nprovider chain, --window, the opt-in --desktop installer flag and the nine\nDESKTOP_* settings by name. USAGE gains five anchored task-shaped subsections\nplus two troubleshooting entries pointing back at the two gotchas most likely\nto be hit. The in-dashboard Guide extends its existing deployment section,\nbecause documentation belongs where the user is and they read the dashboard.\n\nThe awkward facts are stated rather than smoothed over, since each one is a\nquestion somebody will otherwise ask:\n\n- app-mode is the default because three things the dashboard depends on break\n  inside an embedded webview -- window.open (both OAuth logins), <a download>\n  (the analytics export) and navigator.clipboard (every copy button).\n- pywebview ships disabled, and the reason is given rather than hidden.\n- DESKTOP_* settings apply on the NEXT mcc-desktop launch, not to a running\n  tray, because a setting that appears to do nothing is worse than one that is\n  absent.\n- WSL and headless hosts have no tray; mcc-server is the path, reached from a\n  browser on the Windows side.\n- --desktop is opt-in and a default install creates no shortcuts.\n\nVersion bumped because index.html is under src/my_claude_code/api/, which the\nversioning rules count as production even for a text-only change.\n\nVerification: full suite 6 failed, 4377 passed, 26 skipped -- the same six\nPowerShell installer failures, zero new. tests/contracts 192 passed, which\nmatters more than usual here: the new asset-wiring guards mean any class added\nto index.html without a CSS rule now fails, and the Guide edit reuses existing\nguide-note/guide-note-warn classes rather than inventing any. Every pre-existing\nREADME anchor is intact; the diff is 174 insertions and no deletions.\n\nNot verified: the Guide was not rendered in a browser. The markup was checked\nmechanically -- classes resolve to real rules, anchors resolve to real ids --\nwhich is the cheap substitute, not a substitute for looking.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
